# Line-ending policy.
#
# This file exists so that a machine's checkout setting cannot change what the
# repository contains, or what the project's own checks see.
#
# Why it is needed: the project is developed on Windows, where git's
# core.autocrlf rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. Two of the project's checks
# compare a file on disk against content the project generates, and both were
# fooled by that rewrite:
#
#   tools/export-database-dump.mjs --check
#       compared database/charityevents_db.sql against the file it would
#       assemble, and reported STALE on any machine whose checkout differed
#       from the one that generated it.
#
#   tools/sync-theme-snippet.mjs --check
#       compared the inline theme snippet in each page against the one in
#       js/theme.js, and reported every page as OUT OF DATE for the same
#       reason.
#
# In both cases the content was correct and only the line endings differed.
# Pinning the files these checks read makes the comparison independent of the
# checkout. The blobs in the repository are already LF, so this adds no churn;
# it only stops the working copy from being rewritten.

# The database scripts and the dump assembled from them.
database/*.sql text eol=lf

# The pages and the client modules. tools/sync-theme-snippet.mjs reads the
# pages and js/theme.js, and the test suite loads the pages into jsdom.
*.html text eol=lf

# The client and server JavaScript, and the tooling.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf

# Data files read by the tooling.
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

# Batch files are Windows-only, so they keep Windows endings.
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never rewritten.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.zip  binary
*.docx binary
*.pdf  binary